// --- The `piece` curation pass (2026-09-02, owner-ruled; plan 0022) ---------
// SUPERSEDED 2026-09-08 by ADR-0015 — the 143 `{"remove": ["piece"]}` lines
// this section used to hold are DELETED, and the reasoning is recorded here
// rather than in a file of dead rulings.
//
// The pass answered "which rows should not offer `piece`?" by hand, row by
// row: where the vocabulary had a better word — a clove, an avocado, a medium
// potato — `piece` came off the explicit `allowed_units` list, so nobody ever
// had to guess which measure a `piece` meant. It was correct about the
// problem and wrong about the fact. The owner's replacement ruling makes a
// piece weight a ROW FACT like a density: `piece` is admitted iff the row's
// default unit is `piece` AND the row says what one WEIGHS. A `piece` that
// can be weighed converts, totals and shops; one that cannot is not offered.
//
// So the removals are not just retired, they are WRONG under the new rule:
// every one of them names a row that either is not piece-default at all (the
// rule never gave it `piece`) or is piece-default AND weighed below (the rule
// gives it `piece` on purpose). Keeping them would strip `piece` off exactly
// the rows that have earned it.
//
// Three measure edits the owner ruled in the same pass ride along below, and
// they stand — they are about the MEASURES, not about admission.
{"kind": "drop_measure", "match_text": "broccoli", "label": "bunch", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 (\"bunch -> piece\"): the 608 g bunch is what a recipe means by \"1 broccoli\" — relabelled `whole` below so the whole reads as one, which is what lets `piece` come out of the list"}
{"kind": "add_measure", "match_text": "broccoli", "label": "whole", "basis_amount": 608, "sort_order": 0, "source": "usda_fdc:170379 (1 bunch)", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02: the same USDA 1-bunch portion under the name a recipe uses — a whole broccoli. Provenance kept verbatim from the row it replaces; only the label changed"}
{"kind": "drop_measure", "match_text": "cherry tomato", "label": "cherry", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 (\"cherry is wrong... drop piece / cherry / tomato altogether\"): cherry tomatoes are measured by the cup or by weight, and the borrowed generic-tomato `1 cherry` portion only invited a count nobody writes"}
{"kind": "add_measure", "match_text": "ginger", "label": "piece, 1 inch", "basis_amount": 12, "sort_order": 1, "source": "seed:typical", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 asked for a countable piece of ginger; recipes count a 1-inch / thumb-sized piece. USDA's 2.2 g slice is a thin 1\"-diameter round, NOT a 1-inch piece, so this carries its own typical weight (10-15 g band) and AWAITS THE OWNER'S CONFIRMATION — tracked in docs/exec-plans/tech-debt-tracker.md. `slice` stays"}

// --- Piece weights (2026-09-03 pass, re-keyed 2026-09-08 by ADR-0015) ------
// What ONE of this ingredient WEIGHS, in the row's basis unit. These were the
// `default_measure` rulings of seam D1 ("2 onions" -> 2 x `onion, medium`),
// and they carry over verbatim — the same curated size, read now as a NUMBER
// on the row (`piece_basis_amount`, stamped `borrowed from <label>`) instead
// of as a pointer to a measure. Every reason below is the one the owner
// signed off on 2026-09-03; nothing was re-judged, only re-keyed.
//
// The three shapes the pass used: sized family -> the `medium`; sole count
// measure -> that one; fragment set (two or more KINDS of countable thing, no
// dominant one) -> the row was flagged. The full table with the options that
// lost: docs/exec-plans/completed/0024-seam-decisions.md D1.
//
// ADR-0015 narrows the set to the rows the fact is ABOUT — a piece weight is
// only meaningful where the row is counted — so two things happened here:
//
//   * The 65 rulings on rows whose `default_unit` is NOT `piece` were
//     deleted. They named the right measure for a row nobody counts (a can of
//     tomatoes is oz-default; a cup of blueberries is cup-default), and under
//     the new rule they say nothing at all.
//   * Seven of the nine `label: null` flags were on such rows and went with
//     them. Of the two that were piece-default: `red cabbage` takes the whole
//     item it always had (`head, medium`, 839 g) — a leaf is a fragment of
//     one, never one of them; `mint` has no honest whole at all (sprig 2 g vs
//     bunch 25 g, 12x apart), so ITS DEFAULT UNIT MOVES to `g` in vocab.jsonl
//     and the flag is gone. A row that cannot say what one of it weighs is
//     not counted — that is the ruling, said in the vocabulary rather than
//     patched over here.
//
// Every remaining piece-default row therefore carries a weight, which is what
// R3 in seed_curation.sql now asserts.
//
// One seed gap came out of the original pass: `yellow bell pepper` carried
// only `pepper, large` where the other three bells carry medium/large/small,
// so the medium is borrowed from them (119 g, the same weight all three
// share) and the piece weight lands on it.
