//
// --- The round-five vocabulary additions (owner-ruled) ---------------------
// Three rows the vocabulary was missing, each ruled by the owner rather than
// derived: Extra Virgin Olive Oil is its OWN row (not an alias of Olive Oil —
// "they are NOT the same"), Peas is the plain-peas row Frozen Peas was
// standing in for, and Canned Lentils joins the `Canned *` family so a can of
// lentils stops being read against a dry row's macros.
//
// Two of the three carry no usda_links entry, and that is the point:
//   * extra virgin olive oil — FDC's own extra-virgin record (748608) carries
//     a density but NO nutrients in the committed reference, so a link would
//     buy a density and leave the row a macro-less stub. All olive oil is
//     ~100 % fat / 884 kcal per 100 g, so the macros are borrowed from the
//     refined record (171413) with the borrow stated, exactly the way the
//     canned beans borrow pinto's drained basis; the DENSITY is extra
//     virgin's own record, cited fdc_density:748608. The rows are separate
//     because they are different foods to cook with, not because their
//     macros differ.
//   * lentil canned — FDC has no canned-lentil food AT ALL (only raw, dry,
//     boiled and sprouted), so the no-analogue rule keeps the row link-less.
//     Boiled lentils (172421) IS the drained-can basis, so its macros and
//     its portion-derived density are borrowed under the round-2 coherence
//     ruling: the macros must be on the same basis as the can measure they
//     will be multiplied by.
//
// The can weight is the one number here with no FDC portion behind it. No
// canned-lentil food means no `1 can drained solids` row to cite, and the
// bean cans' weights describe a 15 oz US can, not the 400 g European one the
// owner buys. 240 g is the typical printed drained weight for a 400 g can of
// pulses — the same number this file already commits for the green-bean can
// — and it is marked seed:typical so the guess is visible rather than
// dressed as a citation.
